  {
   "type": "grammar",
   "title": "'I am', 'you are': the little endings",
   "md": "Persian usually does not use a separate word for *am / are / is*. Instead a short ending attaches to the adjective or noun:\n\nSo [[خوبم|khub-am]] is one word meaning *I am well*, and [[خوبید|khub-id]] means *you are well*. In writing, the ending is simply joined to the word. The one exception is *he/she/it is*, which is a separate word, [[است|ast]]: [[حال شما چطور است؟|hāl-e shomā chetor ast?|How is your state?]]\n\nIn speech, **ast** shrinks to **-e**: *chetor-e?* You will see the spoken forms marked 🗣 throughout this course.",
   "table": {
    "head": [
     "Persian",
     "Reading",
     "English"
    ],
    "rows": [
     [
      "[[من خوبم|man khub-am]]",
      "-am",
      "I am well"
     ],
     [
      "[[تو خوبی|to khub-i]]",
      "-i",
      "you (one friend) are well"
     ],
     [
      "[[او خوب است|u khub ast]]",
      "ast",
      "he / she is well"
     ],
     [
      "[[ما خوبیم|mā khub-im]]",
      "-im",
      "we are well"
     ],
     [
      "[[شما خوبید|shomā khub-id]]",
      "-id",
      "you (polite / plural) are well"
     ],
     [
      "[[آن‌ها خوبند|ān-hā khub-and]]",
      "-and",
      "they are well"
     ]
    ]
   },
