  {
   "type": "culture",
   "title": "Ta'ārof: the art of politeness",
   "md": "Iranians have a word for their famous politeness: [[تعارف|ta'ārof]]. A shopkeeper may wave away your money with [[قابل ندارد|ghābel nadārad|it's not worthy (of you)]] – you are still expected to pay, and to insist. A host offers food three times; a guest refuses twice. Everyone knows the rules, and the dance is part of the pleasure of talking.\n\nTwo words from the bakery carry this spirit: [[بفرمایید|befarmāyid]] (*please – go ahead, here you are, come in, sit down*) is the all-purpose polite invitation, and [[نوش جان|nush-e jān]] (*may it nourish your soul*) is said to someone about to eat. You will hear both every day.\n\nNotice also [[جان|jān|dear, soul]] after a name: *Rezā jān*. It is warm but not romantic – friends, parents, teachers and shopkeepers all use it.",
   "image": "sangak"
  },
  {
   "type": "tips",
   "title": "Pronunciation",
   "items": [
    "**ā** (as in salām, khodā) is a long, deep 'a' like the vowel in British *father* – keep it long.",
    "**kh** (خ) is the rasping sound of Scottish *loch* or German *Bach*: khub, khodā hāfez.",
    "Stress falls on the **last syllable** of most words: sa-LĀM, kho-DĀ, mam-NUN – but on the ending-less part of a verb: KHUB-am.",
    "Persian 'r' is tapped like Spanish *r*, never swallowed like American *r*."
   ]
  },
